This is a definition of a generic sliding-list selector.  It permits
easily creating and maintaining an area within a window display where
a list of names is shown, allowing selection by rolling the mouse up or
down - scrolling the list when items outside the current display are
desired, and supporting for a scroll bar.  As an option, you can also
position using the cursor keys.

The immense flexibility of this function lies in the fact that unlike
similar requesters, it is not limited to file lists - any data items
chained together as a list of Amiga Nodes will do - you simply supply
the item rendering routine - which can be anything, including routines
that render graphics as well as text.

Like similar selection list functions, this one divides the display
into a series of vertically-stacked selection boxes.  However, UNLIKE
most, they are all a single gadget!  You supply a gadget of the
appropriate size, and the selection-list routine subdivides it, based
on the characteristics of the current font of the RastPort supplied.
There's only one limitation - a maximum of 32 entries are supported in
the display area, because of the mechanism by which I disarm any deleted
nodes which are currently displayed.  That can be changed by modifying the
source code slightly, however.

OBJECTS WHICH MAKE UP THE SYSTEM:

1. A Window

2. A Selector - for actual use, create a new class whose base class is
   a Selector and define a render() function in it.  "render()" is a
   virtual function and must be supplied or the selection gadget fills up
   with "error - no render function supplied"-type messages.  The render
   function can do just about anything, subject to the limitation that you
   put the pieces back together afterwards.  On entry, the RastPort passed
   to render() is set so that a Text() function call will place text in the
   slot to be rendered into.  Graphics are also welcome, as are different
   colors, etc.

3. A SelectionGadget - It wasn't worth the trouble to actually define a
   SelectionGadget class - a SelectionGadget is just a pair of Intuition
   Gadgets - one for the selection subwindow (pane) and one for the scroll
   bar. The scroll bar MUST be pointed to by the NextGadget pointer of the
   selection pane.  It is a Proportional Gadget, and only the vertical axis
   is tracked.  The selection pane is a simple BOOLEAN Gadget - to function
   effectively, the flag GADGHNONE and the activation flags GADGIMMEDIATE,
   FOLLOWMOUSE, and RELVERIFY must be set.

   The Selector construction routine will map the rectangular space
   defined by the gadget's Width and Height into a set of vertically
   stacked slots, which render() will be called upon to fill in sequence.
   The slot height is detemined by the font height parameter in the
   window's RastPort at the time the Selector is constructed, and remains
   constant for the life of the Selector.

   In addition to render(), there is one other virtual function -
   adjust_scrollbar() - it can be used to ModifyProp the scroll bar,
   and is called when the display is initialized, refreshed, or when
   Nodes are added or removed.  This one's optional, the default one is
   what most people are used to seeing, anyway.

4. A List - pointing to a set of Nodes, which can be defined as base classes
   to any sort of structure you like.  render() will receive a pointer to
   these nodes whenever they are to be displayed, and can use the data
   attached to the node (or any other data) to do its rendering with.

Performance criteria:

Pfffftt!  Too much time is spent worrying over nanoseconds.  You CAN'T
keep all those saved nanoseconds in a Mason jar and pour them out when
you need them!  First priority was that the Selector work as reliably
and predictably as possible (considering who the author is), secondly that
it be flexible enough that its insides didn't need major surgery for each
and every program that needed it.  Memory occupancy worried me just a tiny
bit (it's about 6K at last count - peanuts by today's standards.  The
graphics memory it eats up is likely to be far more).  Beyond that, it
gives the proper real-time response to the user's actions, and doesn't
sit too heavily on background tasks, so why turn it into an unreadable
mess just to be ables to brag about efficiency?  So there!

Reliability criteria:

This is MUCH more critical.  The one thing you DON'T do is screw around with
the selection List's innards while it's being displayed.  The basic
selector class defines an append() and remove() function, which permits
the most common operations to be performed safely.  append() will append
your Node to the List, update the display, if required, etc.  remove() will
remove the Node from the List.  it will also ghost and disable the node's
representation if it is currently being displayed.  Once the display is
refreshed, the deleted Node's rendering disappears entirely - the ghosting
is to prevent having the nodes suddenly reshuffle themselves under an unwary
user's mouse.

Admittedly, there are other functions that should be here - i.e. get(),
enqueue(), add(), insert(), but they can be easily added.  Next time around,
I guess - when I make a proper linker library file out of it.

Basic usage:

1. Create a new class based on the Selector, and an instance of that class.
2. Supply routines to build and manage the list of items being selected.
3. Supply a render() routine to render a single selection line.
  This routine can DO anything, but if you render into other
  Selector slots, they may do likewise unto you!
4. Place calls from the IDCMP event dispatcher controlling this window.
  These typically include MOUSEMOVE, MOUSEBUTTONS,
  GADGETDOWN, GADGETUP, and INTUITICKS.
5. have fun!

User-callable functions (and what they're good for):

	Selector (Window *w, register Gadget *xsg, List *xl);

This is the constructor function.  Once you call it, forget about touching
the list nodes until you've invoked delete.  Otherwise you'll probably
lose synchronization, Guru, or both!  Certain IDCMP flags are set when
the user clicks on the selection list box.  They are CLEARED when the
list box is de-activated, so if you were using the MOUSEMOVE, MOUSEBUTTONS,
and/or INTUITICKS flags for other things, reset them after calling the
selection functions for MOUSEBUTTONS or GADGETUP.  Rude, ain't I?

	void display_nodes();

Call this guy to cause the display to be updated.  Usually immediately after
construction, 'cause otherwise all those IDCMP events are going to be
confused!

	void start(USHORT mouse_y_coordinate);

Call on GADGETDOWN in the selector box.

	void end_action(USHORT mouse_y_coordinate, BOOL gadget_released);

Call on GADGETUP from the selector box (TRUE), MOUSEBUTTONS SELECTUP (FALSE)

	void mouse_move(USHORT mouse_y_coordinate);

Call on MOUSEMOVE between start() and end_action()s.

	void scroll_set(int potv);

Call on GADGETUP on scroll bar.

	void Intuitick();

call on INTUITICKS.

	Node *clicked_on();

Call to detect mouse double-click on current item (MOUSEBUTTONS SELECTDOWN).
Returns a pointer to that node, or NULL, if not.

	Node *selected_node()

Call to return pointer to highlighed node if any, or NULL if none.

	virtual void render(RastPort *, Node *);

Supply your own.  On entry, the RastPort is pointing to the slot in which
data for the indicated node is to be rendered.  The cp_x and cp_y RastPort
variables indicate that point.  They needn't be restored before returning.

	virtual void adjust_scrollbar(Gadget *bar, int pot, int body);

Optional.  Allows custom control over how the scroll bar display is
modified (NewModifyProp).  The default is good enough for most cases.

	void append(Node *);	// scrollbar selector
	void remove(Node *);

From the time the selector is created (via new) until it is deleted, these
are the ONLY ways you can add or remove nodes from the list without creating
havoc. DON'T use the general Node routines, use the Selector ones (i.e.
the selector is the object). Others (insert, enqueue) are pending.

Optional cursor-key scrolling.  Amiga conventions (raw keycodes) are:

 up-arrow 	- CURSORUP	- scroll(SELECT_UPWARDS);
 down-arrow	- CURSORDOWN	- scroll(SELECT_DOWNWARDS);
 shift+up	- CURSORUP,  IEQUALIFIER_LSHIFT | IEQUALIFIER+_RSHIFT
			- scroll_page(SELECT_UP)
 shift+down	- can't you guess?
 ALT+up	- CURSORUP, ALTLEFT | ALTRIGHT - scroll_to(SELECT_TOP)
 ALT+down	- CURSORDOWN,  ""		- scroll_to(SELECT_BOTTOM)

 enum scroll_dir { SELECT_DOWNWARDS, SELECT_UPWARDS };

	void scroll(enum scroll_dir);		// 1 line up or down
	void scroll_page(enum scroll_dir);	// display frame up or down.
#define SELECT_TOP 0
#define SELECT_BOTTOM 65535
	void scroll_to(int i);			// Absolute position. 'i'
		is the node number relative to the start of the list (0). 

